1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:14,751
The first country that asked to belong to Albert Wesker
had a population of eleven thousand,

2
00:00:14,771 --> 00:00:21,269
a gross domestic product smaller than a mid-sized
American hospital, and four years of

3
00:00:21,289 --> 00:00:22,330
dry land left.

4
00:00:22,730 --> 00:00:28,630
The Republic of the Line Islands is a real place
with a real parliament and a real seat

5
00:00:28,650 --> 00:00:34,483
at the United Nations, and in 2045 its highest point
was two point nine metres above a

6
00:00:34,503 --> 00:00:40,608
sea that had risen twenty-two centimetres in a decade.
Its government had spent nine years

7
00:00:40,628 --> 00:00:46,393
asking the world for money to build defences and
had been given four per cent of what

8
00:00:46,413 --> 00:00:49,115
it needed, in tranches, with conditions.

9
00:00:49,515 --> 00:00:56,643
In February of 2045 its Prime Minister, a
forty-one-year-old former fisheries lawyer named

10
00:00:56,664 --> 00:01:03,792
Teuea Rabaere, flew to Singapore and asked for a
meeting with Umbrella's regional director

11
00:01:03,812 --> 00:01:07,366
and was given one with Albert
Wesker instead.

12
00:01:07,885 --> 00:01:12,980
I have nothing to offer you. I want to be honest
about that in the first minute, because

13
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,281
everybody else I have met in nine years has
let me talk for an hour first.

14
00:01:17,682 --> 00:01:24,572
You have a seat in the General Assembly, a maritime
exclusive economic zone of seven hundred

15
00:01:24,592 --> 00:01:30,957
thousand square kilometres, and the legal
personality of a sovereign state. Those are

16
00:01:30,977 --> 00:01:37,342
three of the four most valuable things on this
planet, and you are the only person in

17
00:01:37,362 --> 00:01:39,972
that building who does not know it.

18
00:01:40,701 --> 00:01:41,727
And the fourth?

19
00:01:42,128 --> 00:01:46,918
Time, which you do not have, which
is why you are here.

20
00:01:47,317 --> 00:01:52,636
The arrangement they made over the following
fifteen months has been studied in every

21
00:01:52,656 --> 00:01:58,163
school of international law since, and the narrator
will describe it plainly because its

22
00:01:58,183 --> 00:02:00,048
plainness was the whole trick.

23
00:02:00,447 --> 00:02:06,199
Umbrella did not buy the Line Islands. You cannot
buy a country; the attempt would have

24
00:02:06,219 --> 00:02:11,970
been struck down in nine jurisdictions and would
have made him a colonial administrator

25
00:02:11,990 --> 00:02:13,828
in every newspaper on earth.

26
00:02:14,228 --> 00:02:20,632
What Umbrella did was sign a Treaty of Association — an
instrument that exists in international

27
00:02:20,652 --> 00:02:26,651
law, that four real countries already had with larger
neighbours, and that had never once

28
00:02:26,671 --> 00:02:28,478
been signed with a company.

29
00:02:28,877 --> 00:02:34,778
Under it: Umbrella assumed the defence, the coastal
engineering, the power generation,

30
00:02:34,798 --> 00:02:40,904
the health system and the currency of the Republic.
The Republic retained its parliament,

31
00:02:40,924 --> 00:02:47,306
its flag, its seat at the UN, its courts and its laws.
Umbrella got a nineteen-year renewable

32
00:02:47,326 --> 00:02:53,364
lease on four hundred hectares, the right to
incorporate under Line Islands law, and one

33
00:02:53,384 --> 00:02:59,353
clause that the world's foreign ministries spent two
years arguing about: *the Republic

34
00:02:59,373 --> 00:03:05,617
may, by resolution of its parliament, extend the
protection of this treaty to any territory

35
00:03:05,637 --> 00:03:06,856
that requests it.*

36
00:03:07,257 --> 00:03:13,024
The sea wall went up in fourteen months. It is
eleven kilometres long, it is built of

37
00:03:13,044 --> 00:03:19,152
a mineral composite that Birkin's people grew rather
than poured, and it cost four billion

38
00:03:19,172 --> 00:03:24,667
dollars, which was approximately what Umbrella
spent that year on staff catering.

39
00:03:25,067 --> 00:03:31,801
The islands' power became free in 2046. The health
system, which had had four doctors,

40
00:03:31,821 --> 00:03:38,713
had thirty-one by 2047 and a hospital that was better
than anything within four thousand

41
00:03:38,733 --> 00:03:39,576
kilometres.

42
00:03:39,977 --> 00:03:47,262
And in November 2047 the Republic held a referendum — a
real one, monitored by nine international

43
00:03:47,282 --> 00:03:53,663
observers, which Umbrella did not fund — on whether
to make the Treaty of Association

44
00:03:53,683 --> 00:03:54,417
permanent.

45
00:03:54,817 --> 00:04:00,306
The turnout was ninety-one per cent. The result was
seventy-four per cent in favour.

46
00:04:01,456 --> 00:04:08,569
Territory: protectorate. The Republic of the Line
Islands, population eleven thousand

47
00:04:08,589 --> 00:04:15,702
four hundred. Instrument: a Treaty of Association
ratified by referendum. Sovereignty

48
00:04:15,722 --> 00:04:23,170
retained by the Republic. Sentiment: ninety-one per
cent favourable. Nine million Points,

49
00:04:23,190 --> 00:04:30,554
recurring. Note: this is the first population that
chose you. I did not place them. They

50
00:04:30,574 --> 00:04:31,057
voted.

51
00:04:31,648 --> 00:04:37,696
The world's reaction took three years and went
through four stages, and the narrator will

52
00:04:37,716 --> 00:04:43,559
name them because the same four stages recur, at
larger scale, every time for the next

53
00:04:43,579 --> 00:04:44,378
forty years.

54
00:04:44,778 --> 00:04:51,008
The first was ridicule. A company with a
protectorate; a corporate flag; eleven thousand

55
00:04:51,028 --> 00:04:53,849
people on an atoll. There were cartoons.

56
00:04:54,249 --> 00:05:00,777
The second was legal panic, which began when the
Marshall Islands opened negotiations

57
00:05:00,797 --> 00:05:08,018
in 2046, and turned into something else entirely when
Fiji — population nine hundred thousand,

58
00:05:08,038 --> 00:05:11,639
a genuine state with an army —
asked for terms.

59
00:05:12,038 --> 00:05:18,399
The third was the counter-offer. Between 2046 and
2049 the United States, the European

60
00:05:18,419 --> 00:05:24,779
Union, China, Australia and Japan collectively
committed one hundred and forty billion

61
00:05:24,799 --> 00:05:31,234
dollars in climate adaptation finance to the
Pacific, which was twenty-seven times what

62
00:05:31,254 --> 00:05:35,389
they had provided in the previous
twenty years combined.

63
00:05:35,788 --> 00:05:41,528
The narrator asks you to notice that this happened,
that it was good, that four million

64
00:05:41,548 --> 00:05:47,419
people's lives were materially improved by it, and
that it happened solely because Albert

65
00:05:47,439 --> 00:05:51,259
Wesker had made it clear that if they
would not, he would.

66
00:05:51,658 --> 00:05:57,443
The fourth stage was accommodation. By 2049 the
Treaty of Association had been signed

67
00:05:57,463 --> 00:06:03,589
by four states and was being negotiated by nine. The
General Assembly debated a resolution

68
00:06:03,609 --> 00:06:10,213
declaring such treaties incompatible with the Charter. It
failed, sixty-one votes to ninety-four,

69
00:06:10,233 --> 00:06:16,086
because ninety-four countries had worked out that
the resolution's real purpose was to

70
00:06:16,106 --> 00:06:19,569
stop poor states from choosing
their own protector.

71
00:06:19,968 --> 00:06:25,716
The delegate from the Line Islands spoke for four
minutes in that debate and the narrator

72
00:06:25,736 --> 00:06:31,354
will quote the end of it, because it is the single
most damaging thing anyone ever said

73
00:06:31,374 --> 00:06:34,918
about the international system and
because it was true.

74
00:06:35,439 --> 00:06:40,795
For nine years I stood in this chamber and asked
this body for twenty-two centimetres

75
00:06:40,815 --> 00:06:46,299
of concrete. I was given speeches. I was given
frameworks. I was given a working group.

76
00:06:46,579 --> 00:06:51,822
A company came, and built a wall, and did not ask me
to vote a particular way, and has

77
00:06:51,842 --> 00:06:57,207
not asked me for anything since. You may tell me this
is dangerous. I am sure it is. But

78
00:06:57,227 --> 00:07:02,838
nobody in this room has ever offered my people an
alternative, and I would ask the delegates

79
00:07:02,858 --> 00:07:08,040
who are about to vote for this resolution to name
the year in which they intended to.

80
00:07:08,880 --> 00:07:09,990
Nobody did.

81
00:07:10,391 --> 00:07:16,549
The sea wall is worth four paragraphs, because it
is the only thing Umbrella built in

82
00:07:16,569 --> 00:07:20,983
the 2040s that was not placed, and
because of what that cost.

83
00:07:21,382 --> 00:07:26,590
The System would have put it there in a night for
about nine hundred thousand Points,

84
00:07:26,610 --> 00:07:31,879
complete, with eleven years of maintenance records
and a tender process that had never

85
00:07:31,899 --> 00:07:32,432
happened.

86
00:07:32,833 --> 00:07:38,749
Wesker paid instead: four billion dollars, fourteen
months, thirteen hundred workers,

87
00:07:38,769 --> 00:07:44,825
of whom six hundred were from the islands and were
trained for it at Umbrella's expense

88
00:07:44,845 --> 00:07:50,063
at a school Umbrella built for the purpose and
then handed to the Republic.

89
00:07:50,463 --> 00:07:58,533
Catherine Adeyemi asked him why, in writing, because by
2045 she asked everything in writing.

90
00:07:59,052 --> 00:08:03,280
We are spending four billion dollars and fourteen
months on an asset we could have for

91
00:08:03,300 --> 00:08:07,577
nine hundred thousand Points and no time at all.
Explain the difference, because I have

92
00:08:07,597 --> 00:08:08,121
to sign it.

93
00:08:08,521 --> 00:08:13,731
The difference is six hundred islanders who will
know how to build a sea wall.

94
00:08:14,130 --> 00:08:15,360
That is sentiment.

95
00:08:15,761 --> 00:08:22,027
(very cold) It is *infrastructure*. In eleven years
the sea will be higher than this wall

96
00:08:22,047 --> 00:08:28,102
and it will need another one, and I intend for the
people who build that one to be the

97
00:08:28,122 --> 00:08:34,319
grandchildren of the people who built this one, and
for none of them ever to have to ask

98
00:08:34,339 --> 00:08:34,531
me.

99
00:08:35,140 --> 00:08:41,971
I have placed four cities, Ms. Adeyemi, and I will
place a great many more. Every one

100
00:08:41,991 --> 00:08:49,306
of them arrives with the skills already inside it, in
people who cannot teach them, because

101
00:08:49,326 --> 00:08:55,350
the knowledge came with the paperwork and does
not live in anybody's hands.

102
00:08:55,630 --> 00:09:02,366
(very cold) Teraina is the first thing I have ever
built that leaves something behind

103
00:09:02,386 --> 00:09:03,161
when I go.

104
00:09:03,561 --> 00:09:09,789
The wall was finished in 2047. In 2071 the Republic
built the second one itself, with

105
00:09:09,809 --> 00:09:16,037
its own money, using a contractor from the islands,
and Umbrella's involvement was an

106
00:09:16,057 --> 00:09:21,697
invoice for four hundred thousand dollars of
materials that was paid in full.

107
00:09:22,096 --> 00:09:27,680
The narrator notes that Albert Wesker flew out for
the opening of the second wall and

108
00:09:27,700 --> 00:09:33,678
was not on the platform, and that the photographs of
that day show a row of local engineers

109
00:09:33,698 --> 00:09:39,281
and, at the back of the crowd, a man in a black
coat that nobody in the photograph is

110
00:09:39,301 --> 00:09:40,006
looking at.

111
00:09:40,407 --> 00:09:46,136
Carrick went out there in 2049 because he did not
believe it, and he spent eleven days

112
00:09:46,156 --> 00:09:50,817
on the island with a notebook, and he came
back with the fifth folder.

113
00:09:51,336 --> 00:09:52,937
I couldn't find anything.

114
00:09:53,336 --> 00:09:55,027
You sound disappointed.

115
00:09:55,427 --> 00:10:01,075
(flat) I am, and I've spent three weeks working out
why, and it's this. There is no coercion

116
00:10:01,095 --> 00:10:06,744
on that island. The hospital is real, the wall is real,
the referendum was clean — I watched

117
00:10:06,764 --> 00:10:12,167
the observers, I interviewed four of them. Ninety-one
per cent turnout. Nobody was paid,

118
00:10:12,187 --> 00:10:17,466
nobody was threatened, and the Prime Minister argues
with your regional director about

119
00:10:17,486 --> 00:10:20,916
fishing quotas every month and wins
about half the time.

120
00:10:21,197 --> 00:10:26,976
And in eleven days I did not meet a single person
who could tell me what they would do

121
00:10:26,996 --> 00:10:28,797
if they changed their mind.

122
00:10:29,581 --> 00:10:37,505
They would hold another referendum. The treaty is
terminable on two years' notice by resolution

123
00:10:37,525 --> 00:10:44,612
of their parliament. I insisted on that clause;
their negotiators did not ask for it.

124
00:10:45,011 --> 00:10:50,666
I know. I read it. And then I asked eleven people
on that island whether they thought

125
00:10:50,686 --> 00:10:56,408
their parliament would ever vote to give up free
power, a hospital and a sea wall, and

126
00:10:56,428 --> 00:10:59,011
every single one of them laughed at me.

127
00:10:59,292 --> 00:11:04,976
That's the thing you've built. Not a cage. A *door
that nobody will ever walk through*.

128
00:11:04,996 --> 00:11:09,042
It's voluntary in exactly the way that
breathing is voluntary.

129
00:11:09,442 --> 00:11:13,492
They walked along the wall for a while with
the sea on one side of it.

130
00:11:14,011 --> 00:11:20,201
Mr. Carrick, is there a difference between that
and any government on earth?

131
00:11:20,601 --> 00:11:26,296
Yes. The difference is that a government is a thing
people can throw out in an afternoon

132
00:11:26,316 --> 00:11:32,205
and that has to survive being hated. You have built
something that cannot be hated, because

133
00:11:32,225 --> 00:11:38,050
it has never once done anything hateful, and you have
therefore removed the only mechanism

134
00:11:38,070 --> 00:11:41,232
our species has ever had for
correcting anything.

135
00:11:41,951 --> 00:11:47,133
I'm not going to write this one up. There's no
finding. It's just the shape of it, and

136
00:11:47,153 --> 00:11:50,761
the shape is the problem, and I don't
have a recommendation.

137
00:11:51,162 --> 00:11:53,451
(very cold) Write it anyway.

138
00:11:53,851 --> 00:11:54,481
Why?

139
00:11:54,881 --> 00:12:01,399
Because in about forty years somebody is going to ask
how this happened, and I would prefer

140
00:12:01,419 --> 00:12:06,932
that the answer be in the handwriting of the
one man who told me at the time.

141
00:12:07,331 --> 00:12:13,576
The fifth folder is four pages long. It is the only
one in the drawer with no annotation

142
00:12:13,596 --> 00:12:14,502
on it at all.

143
00:12:14,902 --> 00:12:18,182
And then, in 2050, Umbrella built its own.

144
00:12:18,581 --> 00:12:24,469
The Arcology was placed — not built; placed, over
the night of the eleventh of June, in

145
00:12:24,489 --> 00:12:30,241
four hundred metres of water thirteen hundred
kilometres from the nearest coast — and

146
00:12:30,261 --> 00:12:34,111
it is the largest single object the
System ever gave him.

147
00:12:35,261 --> 00:12:43,118
Territory: the Arcology. An ocean platform city with
a capacity of four hundred thousand,

148
00:12:43,138 --> 00:12:51,171
currently sixty-one thousand resident. Four fusion
units. Unlimited desalination. A charter

149
00:12:51,191 --> 00:12:58,782
written by you. A currency issued by you. Legal
basis: none whatsoever. Note: this one

150
00:12:58,802 --> 00:13:04,091
has no parliament. Consider carefully
what you are building.

151
00:13:04,682 --> 00:13:10,532
Sixty-one thousand people moved there in the first
year: researchers, engineers, their

152
00:13:10,552 --> 00:13:16,335
families, and about nine thousand who simply
applied because the housing was free and

153
00:13:16,355 --> 00:13:18,451
the schools were extraordinary.

154
00:13:18,851 --> 00:13:24,913
Twenty-nine states recognised it as a legal entity
within four months, not because they

155
00:13:24,933 --> 00:13:30,854
approved, but because the alternative was to have
sixty-one thousand stateless people

156
00:13:30,874 --> 00:13:37,075
and eight fusion reactors floating in international
water with no legal framework at all.

157
00:13:37,475 --> 00:13:43,645
The first child born a citizen of the Umbrella Free
Territory arrived on the fourth of

158
00:13:43,665 --> 00:13:50,195
September 2050, in a hospital that was better than any
on land, to a Bangladeshi structural

159
00:13:50,215 --> 00:13:54,945
engineer and a Peruvian marine biologist
who had met in a canteen.

160
00:13:55,346 --> 00:14:01,466
Her name was Mira. She is nineteen when the sky
opens, and she is still alive at the end

161
00:14:01,486 --> 00:14:07,676
of this account, and she is one of the eleven people
who sees what Albert Wesker becomes.
